1246	\begin{proof}
1247		\uses{def:cl-func}
1248		Since an $\ell$-level CL function is also $k$-level conditionally linear for all $k \ge \ell$, it suffices to prove the claim when $\ell_j = \ell$ for all $j$. Proceed by induction on $\ell$. For $\ell = 0$ every $L^{(j)}$ is the zero function on $V^{(j)}$, so $L$ is the zero function on $V$, which is the unique $0$-level CL function on $V$ (Definition~\ref{def:cl-func}). For the inductive step with $\ell \ge 1$, decompose each $L^{(j)}$ into its first-level linear part $L^{(j)}_1$ on $V^{(j)}_1$ and its family of $(\ell-1)$-level remainders $L^{(j)}_{>1,\,v_j}$ on $V^{(j)}_{>1}$. The direct sums of the first-level parts define a linear map on $V_1 = \bigoplus_j V^{(j)}_1$, and, for each prefix value $v = \sum_j v_j$, the direct sums of the remainders are $(\ell-1)$-level conditionally linear on $V_{>1} = \bigoplus_j V^{(j)}_{>1}$ by the induction hypothesis. This exhibits $L$ in the form required by Definition~\ref{def:cl-func}.
1249	\end{proof}
